## Testing - Verify Actuator is Secured

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.boot.test.autoconfigure.web.servlet.AutoConfigureMockMvc;
import org.springframework.boot.test.context.SpringBootTest;
import org.springframework.test.web.servlet.MockMvc;
import static org.springframework.test.web.servlet.request.MockMvcRequestBuilders.*;
import static org.springframework.test.web.servlet.result.MockMvcResultMatchers.*;
import static org.springframework.security.test.web.servlet.request.SecurityMockMvcRequestPostProcessors.*;

@SpringBootTest
@AutoConfigureMockMvc
public class ActuatorSecurityTests {

    @Autowired
    private MockMvc mockMvc;

    @Test
    public void testEnvEndpointBlocked() throws Exception {
        // SECURE: /actuator/env should be disabled
        mockMvc.perform(get("/actuator/env"))
            .andExpect(status().isNotFound());  // Returns 404, not 200
    }

    @Test
    public void testBeansEndpointRequiresAuth() throws Exception {
        // SECURE: /actuator/beans requires authentication
        mockMvc.perform(get("/actuator/beans"))
            .andExpect(status().isUnauthorized());  // 401, not 200
    }

    @Test
    public void testMetricsWithValidAuth() throws Exception {
        // SECURE: Metrics accessible with valid credentials
        mockMvc.perform(get("/actuator/prometheus")
            .with(httpBasic("metrics_user", "secret_password")))
            .andExpect(status().isOk());
    }

    @Test
    public void testHealthPublicAccess() throws Exception {
        // SECURE: Health endpoint is public but shows limited details
        mockMvc.perform(get("/actuator/health"))
            .andExpect(status().isOk())
            .andExpect(jsonPath("$.status").exists())
            .andExpect(jsonPath("$.components").doesNotExist());  // No internal details
    }
}
```

## Security Checklist

✅ **Endpoint Exposure**
- [ ] Remove `exposure.include: "*"`
- [ ] Use explicit whitelist (only `health` and `prometheus`)
- [ ] Disable `env`, `beans`, `mappings`, `loggers` endpoints
- [ ] Disable `shutdown` endpoint entirely

✅ **Authentication & Authorization**
- [ ] Enable Spring Security (don't exclude SecurityAutoConfiguration)
- [ ] Require authentication for all actuator endpoints except `/health`
- [ ] Use role-based access control (METRICS, ADMIN)
- [ ] Implement strong password policies

✅ **Secrets Management**
- [ ] Remove secrets from environment variables
- [ ] Use external config server (Spring Cloud Config or Vault)
- [ ] Use role-based database accounts (read-only for queries)
- [ ] Rotate secrets regularly

✅ **Network Security**
- [ ] Use Kubernetes NetworkPolicy to restrict access
- [ ] Allow Prometheus scraping only from monitoring namespace
- [ ] Require HTTPS for all actuator traffic
- [ ] Disable HTTP (requiresSecure)

✅ **Monitoring & Alerting**
- [ ] Log all actuator access attempts
- [ ] Alert on repeated failed authentication
- [ ] Monitor for access to disabled endpoints
- [ ] Track credential usage patterns

✅ **Testing**
- [ ] Test that public endpoints return limited info
- [ ] Test that private endpoints require authentication
- [ ] Test that invalid credentials are rejected
- [ ] Perform penetration testing before production

## Key Lessons

**NEVER**: Disable Spring Security for development convenience
**NEVER**: Expose `env` endpoint to the internet
**NEVER**: Store secrets in environment variables
**NEVER**: Use default/weak credentials for admin access
**ALWAYS**: Test security configuration before production
**ALWAYS**: Use external secret management in production
**ALWAYS**: Monitor and audit actuator access"
